, A Vewe of the Present State of Irelande […], Dublin: […] Societie of Stationers, […], →OCLC; republished as A View of the State of Ireland […] (Ancient Irish Histories), Dublin: […] Society of Stationers, […] Hibernia Press, […] [b]y John Morrison, 1809, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "I was but two bows wide.", "reference": "1656, Thomas Middleton, William Rowley, Philip Massinger, The Old Law:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(phonetics, dated) Made, as a vowel, with a less tense, and more open and relaxed, condition of the organs in the mouth.", "tags": ["dated"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(Scotland, Northern England, now rare) Vast, great in extent, extensive.", "tags": ["Northern England", "Scotland", "archaic"], "examples": ["The wide, lifeless expanse."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) Located some distance away; distant, far.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Mr Hunt's house, you know, lies wide from Harlowe-place.", "reference": "1748, [Samuel Richardson], “Letter LXXXI”, in Clarissa. Or, The History of a Young Lady: […], volume (please specify |volume=I to VII), London: […] S[amuel] Richardson; […], →OCLC:"}, {"text": "the contrary [being] so wide from the truth of Scripture and the attributes of God", "reference": "1654, H[enry] Hammond, Of Fundamentals in a Notion Referring to Practise, London: […] J[ames] Flesher for Richard Royston, […], →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) Far from truth, propriety, necessity, etc.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "And I trust anon by the help of an infallible guide, to perfect such Prutenic tables, as shall mend the astronomy of our wide expositors.", "reference": "1643, J[ohn] M[ilton], The Doctrine and Discipline of Divorce: […], London: […] T[homas] P[aine] and M[atthew] S[immons] […], →OCLC:"}, {"text": "But I tell you, it is farre wyde, that the people haue ſuche iudgmentes, the Byſhoppes they coulde laughe at it.", "reference": "1549 April 22 (Gregorian calendar), Hughe Latymer [i.e., Hugh Latimer], Augustine Bernher, compiler, “[27 Sermons Preached by the Ryght Reuerende Father in God and Constant Matir of Iesus Christe, Maister Hugh Latimer, […].] The Syxte Sermon of Maister Hugh Latymer, whiche He Preached before K. Edward [VI], the XII. Day of Aprill.”, in Certayn Godly Sermons, Made uppon the Lords Prayer, […], London: […] John Day, […], published 1562, →OCLC, folio 75, verso:"}, {"text": "How wide is all this long pretence!", "reference": "[1633], George Herbert, edited by [Nicholas Ferrar], The Temple. Sacred Poems, and Private Ejaculations, Cambridge, Cambridgeshire: […] Thomas Buck and Roger Daniel; and are to be sold by Francis Green, […], →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(computing) Of or supporting a greater range of text characters than can fit into the traditional 8-bit representation.", "tags": [], "examples": ["a wide character; a wide stream"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(British, slang, only in \"wide boy\") Sharp-witted.", "tags": ["British", "slang"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "But the first visitor to penetrate from the outside world proved to be Sergeant Williams; large and pink and scrubbed-looking; and for a little while Grant forgot about battles long ago and considered wide boys alive today.", "reference": "1951, Josephine Tey, The Daughter of Time, page 31:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["broad", "thick", "wide"], "antonyms": ["narrow", "thin", "skinny", "long", "scrawny"]}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name":